# Topic permissions, mirroring what production enforces.
#
# This file is the local expression of docs/device-protocol.md §1.3. HiveMQ
# Cloud and EMQX express the same thing as per-credential permissions with
# identity substitution; AWS IoT Core expresses it as a policy attached to the
# certificate, with `${iot:Connection.Thing.ThingName}` — which is the
# strongest version, because a stolen certificate used from a different client
# id fails to connect at all.

# --- The API: one privileged server client, never a device identity ----------
#
# Note the asymmetry. The server may write to any command topic and read any
# report, and **cannot write** to ack/state/status. There is no reason for the
# server to forge a device report, so it is denied the ability — which means a
# compromised API credential cannot fabricate the security record of a physical
# safe, only issue commands it could issue anyway.
user safelock-api-local
topic write safelock/v1/+/cmd
topic read safelock/v1/+/ack
topic read safelock/v1/+/state
topic read safelock/v1/+/status

# --- Devices -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A device may publish ONLY to its own ack/state/status and subscribe ONLY to
# its own cmd. `%u` is Mosquitto's substitution for the authenticated username,
# which for a device is its serial number — the same value as its client id and
# (in production) its certificate CN, so one identity drives the whole ACL and
# there is no lookup table to get out of sync.
#
# Expressed as a pattern rather than per-device stanzas, so adding a device to
# the local fleet is a password-file entry and not an ACL edit.
#
# This is what signatures alone would not give us: device A holds a valid key
# of its own and could otherwise publish a correctly-signed message to device
# B's ack topic.
pattern read safelock/v1/%u/cmd
pattern write safelock/v1/%u/ack
pattern write safelock/v1/%u/state
pattern write safelock/v1/%u/status
